@marclou I guess you know a good provider?

I've posted on LinkedIn for 4 years now, over 1,500 posts. It's still where my B2B clients come from.

175K+ people follow me there. And at ContentPath ($1M ARR), we do the same for other B2B teams with content and warm outbound.

LinkedIn changed its algorithm this year. I experimented a lot and found what still works on the new feed.

Let me show you the 3 rules that matter now to keep attracting clients:

1/ The quality bar

There are 4 levels of content quality:

- Level 4 is AI slop: generic AI posts that repeat what everyone already says;
- Level 3 is templated content: clean and correct, but the same formats, hooks and visuals as everyone else;
- Level 2 is expert content: real expertise, proof from your work and a clear point of view;
- Level 1 is the 1% content: a fresh piece of content in a signature format and design that only your brand could publish.

Between levels 3 and 2, there is what I call "the quality chasm". Most brands that publish never cross this chasm and stay stuck in levels 3 and 4.

The test for every content idea: "If I removed my name from this post, would my audience still know it's me?"

2/ The formats

With so much AI content, design is one of the few things that still makes you stand out.

The formats I use on my own profile every month:

- Screenshots: they work because they feel authentic, people are tired of polished content;
- Infographics: one visual that summarizes a framework;
- Carousels: at least 10 slides to increase the dwell time;
- Photo + text: a candid photo cuts through the noise.

3/ The new mix

You can't publish only educational content anymore. It was possible before, but the rules have changed.

- Educational content: 40%;
- Thought leadership: 15%;
- Build in public: 15%;
- Personal brand: 15%;
- Offer content: 15%.

Now don't get me wrong, 40% is still a lot. But concentrate the value in dense content (articles repurposed into posts).
